    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d436">

                <pb facs="0455" n="357" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg357"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/242: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Perkins</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-01-03T16:00:00+08:00">January 3, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 3—2:25 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>10. [For the first part of this telegram, see <ref
                        target="frus:frus1929v02/pg_674"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1929, volume II, page 674</ref>, footnote 57.]</p>
                <p>2. At meeting yesterday of Diplomatic Body when mandate of December 28th was
                    informally discussed, I took occasion to say that my instructions were to the
                    effect that the American Government maintained the stand taken in its note of
                    November 1st last<anchor n="42" xml:id="d436-f1"/> that the Department was at
                    present engaged in discussions with the Chinese Minister with a view to the
                    ultimate conclusion of an agreement and proposed to continue the discussion of
                    the subject. I also said that I had received no instructions as to the
                    intentions of my Government with respect to the making of a protest or
                    reservation concerning the mandate in question.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
